<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Make a Poster! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Following Instructions at School</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Make a Poster!</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write thank-you cards. Interactive: Respond to written questions about a class reading assignment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a simple thank-you card to a classmate or teacher for helping with a class project (e.g., "Thank you for helping me cut the paper for the poster."). Can answer simple written questions about steps in a class project (e.g., "What did you cut?" "I cut the paper for the poster." "What did you use to glue the pictures?" "I used glue."). Can write short answers about following instructions in class (e.g., "Where do we have to put the poster?" "On the wall in the classroom." "What did you do after cutting the paper?" "I glued it on the poster.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different craft items (poster, mask, card) and materials (paper, scissors, glue, crayon, paint). Students identify the items by writing their names on small whiteboards or notebooks. The teacher asks, "What do we need to make a poster?" Students write ideas (e.g., "paper," "glue").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple instruction on the board, like "First, cut the paper." then "Next, paste it on the poster." The teacher models writing a short thank-you note: "Thank you for helping." The teacher explicitly pronounces /p/ in "paper" and "poster" and /s/ in "scissors," encouraging students to notice the sounds as they write the words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we say 'Thank you' when someone helps us?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'First, cut the paper,' what is the first thing I do?" (Expected answer: "Cut the paper!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Following Instructions at School".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple written flowchart or a numbered list of instructions for a mini-craft project, like "How to Make a Paper Decoration." The instructions use vocabulary: paper, scissors, glue, cut, fold, paste. Example instructions: "1. Get colorful paper. 2. Fold the paper. 3. Cut small shapes. 4. Paste the shapes on the card." Also, a sample thank-you card message for a classmate. The teacher points out compound words like "classroom" and encourages students to write them as "class-room" to recognize syllable division.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and identifying key information in written instructions and simple cards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Writing focus):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the written instructions for the "Paper Decoration" craft.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks written gist questions on the board or a worksheet: "What are these instructions about?" (Students write: "Making a paper decoration."). "What do you need first?" (Students write: "Colorful paper.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students answer true/false written statements based on the instructions (e.g., "True or False: First, we paste the paper." Students write "False.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Plural of the imperatives ("Let's make a poster."), Sequencing words ("first, next"), Future tense ("I will make a mask."). Vocabulary: paper, scissors, glue, pencil, poster, classroom, cut, fold, paste, draw, write.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences about making a poster, containing blanks. Students write the missing sequencing words (first, next) or target vocabulary words (paper, glue, scissors, cut, paste). Example: "_____, get the poster. _____, glue the pictures." or "I need _____ and _____ to make a poster."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Reorder and Write:</b> Students receive strips of paper or a worksheet with jumbled sentences describing steps for a simple task related to making a poster (e.g., "the paper / cut / First," "the pictures / glue / Next"). Students reorder the words to write correct, complete instructions in their notebooks. They also complete sentences like "I will make a _____ for the project." or "I am going to _____."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Writing & Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students write three simple instructions for how to decorate a card using two colors and one shape. (e.g., "First, draw a big circle. Next, color it red."). They then exchange their instructions with their partner. The partner reads the instructions and writes one question asking for clarification, like "What do I need to do next?" or "What color is the circle?". The original writer then writes an answer.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Following Instructions at School".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual writing of thank-you cards and short written responses to questions about a class project.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Write a Thank-You Card:</b> Each student writes a simple thank-you card to a classmate or the teacher for helping with the "Let's Make a Poster!" project. Students must include their name and a specific detail about the help received. Examples: "Dear [Name], Thank you for helping me cut the paper for the poster. From, [Student's Name]." or "Dear Teacher, Thank you for helping us make the big poster. From, [Student's Name]." (Focus on writing simple, clear sentences with target vocabulary and grammar).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Respond to Project Questions:</b> The teacher projects or writes three simple questions related to the "Let's Make a Poster!" project on the board. Students write short answers in their notebooks.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example Questions:</li>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you cut for the poster?" (Students write: "I cut the paper.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you use to glue the pictures?" (Students write: "I used glue.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where do we have to put the poster?" (Students write: "On the wall in the classroom.")</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to use future tense if they mention next steps: "Next, I will make a decoration."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist for written output, and peer assessment of thank-you cards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their thank-you cards and their written answers to the project questions. Students exchange their thank-you cards with a partner. The partner uses a simple checklist to verify: "Is there a 'Thank you'?" "Is there a name?" "Is there a reason?" (Yes/No check). Students can write a small "Good job!" or "Needs help with..." on their partner's card.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Writing):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and completeness of the thank-you message (e.g., "Thank you for helping me cut...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target vocabulary (paper, scissors, glue, poster, cut, paste).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to write simple, short answers to questions about project steps using sequencing words (e.g., "I cut the paper first.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Basic sentence structure (Subject-Verb-Object) for A1.3.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts in writing on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write a thank-you card to..." (Students complete the sentence).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write 2 words about making a poster:" (Students write two words, e.g., "paper, glue").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b> Students reflect in writing:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult to write today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., "Students were able to write basic thank-you notes with specific details.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to improve writing skills? (e.g., "Provide more sentence frames for future tense practice," "Spend more time on spelling of 'scissors' and 'classroom'.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This writing lesson prepared students to write instructions and short descriptive texts. In the next lesson (Mediation & Integration), students will use these writing skills to summarize information about making their poster project and present it, integrating the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>